WEBVTT

00:00:01.934 --> 00:00:36.276
The Elephant's Child by Rudyard Kipling. Part One: The Elephant's Child Wants Some Answers.

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:40.696
In the beginning, elephants had noses.

00:00:40.756 --> 00:00:47.138
They had large noses, but they could not pick things up with them.

00:00:47.198 --> 00:00:53.169
One day, the elephant's child began to ask everybody questions.

00:00:53.229 --> 00:00:57.770
He always asked a lot of questions.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:04.850
He asked his aunt, the ostrich, Why have you got long feathers?

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:09.192
The ostrich was angry with the elephant's child.

00:01:09.252 --> 00:01:12.773
She punished him and sent him away.

00:01:12.832 --> 00:01:16.304
Don't ask questions, elephant's child.

00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:18.134
She told him.

00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:24.006
The elephant's child then went to his uncle, the giraffe.

00:01:24.066 --> 00:01:26.887
Why have you got spots?

00:01:26.947 --> 00:01:29.407
He asked the giraffe.

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:34.479
His uncle the giraffe was angry with the elephant's child.

00:01:34.538 --> 00:01:38.530
He punished him and sent him away.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:44.024
Don't ask questions, elephant's child.

00:01:44.084 --> 00:01:46.126
He told him.

00:01:46.186 --> 00:01:52.228
The elephant's child now went to his aunt, the hippopotamus.

00:01:52.288 --> 00:01:55.210
Why have you got red eyes?

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.180
He asked his aunt.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.953
The hippopotamus was angry and she too punished the elephant's child.

00:02:04.013 --> 00:02:06.624
Don't ask questions.

00:02:06.684 --> 00:02:08.426
She told him.

00:02:08.486 --> 00:02:14.638
The elephant's child now went to his uncle, the baboon.

00:02:14.698 --> 00:02:17.420
Why do you like melons?

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.671
He asked.

00:02:18.731 --> 00:02:21.032
Why are they good to eat?

00:02:21.092 --> 00:02:26.918
The baboon was angry and he punished the elephant's child.

00:02:26.978 --> 00:02:28.542
Don't

00:02:28.602 --> 00:02:30.843
ask questions,

00:02:30.903 --> 00:02:32.644
elephant's child!

00:02:32.704 --> 00:02:34.974
He told him.

00:02:35.034 --> 00:02:40.346
One day The Elephant's Child had a new question.

00:02:40.406 --> 00:02:43.177
What does the crocodile eat?

00:02:43.237 --> 00:02:45.648
He asked everybody.

00:02:45.708 --> 00:02:49.879
Be quiet elephant's child!

00:02:49.939 --> 00:02:52.772
Everybody shouted at him.

00:02:52.832 --> 00:02:57.776
His uncles and aunts were angry with the elephant's child.

00:02:57.836 --> 00:03:01.578
They all punished him for a long time.

00:03:01.638 --> 00:03:04.898
He was very unhappy.

00:03:04.958 --> 00:03:10.571
He went away for a walk in the forest.

00:03:10.631 --> 00:03:14.493
Part 2.

00:03:14.553 --> 00:03:26.638
The Great, Grey, Green Limpopo River The elephant's child met the Kolokolo bird in the forest.

00:03:26.698 --> 00:03:30.372
He told the Kolokolo bird everything.

00:03:30.432 --> 00:03:38.057
My father punished me, my uncles punished me, and my aunts punished me.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:42.340
He said, What does the crocodile eat?

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.582
Do you know?

00:03:44.642 --> 00:03:48.255
The Kolokolo bird thought for a moment.

00:03:48.315 --> 00:03:58.398
Then he said, Do you know the great, grey, green, Limpopo River?

00:03:58.458 --> 00:03:59.569
Yes.

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:02.110
replied the elephant's child.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:04.241
I know it.

00:04:04.301 --> 00:04:06.462
Go there.

00:04:06.522 --> 00:04:10.484
The Kolokolo bird told the elephant's child.

00:04:10.544 --> 00:04:13.936
You can ask there.

00:04:13.996 --> 00:04:16.867
I'll go there immediately.

00:04:16.927 --> 00:04:19.969
The elephant's child decided.

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:30.612
The elephant's child walked through the forest until he came to the great, grey, green Limpopo River.

00:04:30.672 --> 00:04:34.634
Then he saw a python snake.

00:04:34.694 --> 00:04:36.564
Excuse me?

00:04:36.624 --> 00:04:37.804
He said.

00:04:37.864 --> 00:04:40.975
But is there a crocodile here?

00:04:41.035 --> 00:04:42.866
Yes.

00:04:42.926 --> 00:04:45.296
The python snake told him.

00:04:45.356 --> 00:04:48.758
A crocodile lives here.

00:04:48.818 --> 00:04:50.529
Good!

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.710
replied the elephant's child.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.901
What does he eat?

00:04:54.961 --> 00:05:00.774
The python was angry now, and he punished the elephant's child.

00:05:00.834 --> 00:05:05.546
Don't ask questions, elephant's child.

00:05:05.606 --> 00:05:07.506
He told him.

00:05:07.566 --> 00:05:11.749
The elephant's child walked some more.

00:05:11.808 --> 00:05:15.280
He walked on something big.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.050
It was the crocodile!

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:21.872
The elephant's child looked down.

00:05:21.932 --> 00:05:24.384
Where is the crocodile?

00:05:24.444 --> 00:05:26.806
He asked politely.

00:05:26.866 --> 00:05:32.050
The crocodile opened one eye very slowly.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:36.014
He looked at the elephant's child.

00:05:36.074 --> 00:05:42.330
Come here, elephant's child.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.122
He said quietly.

00:05:45.182 --> 00:05:48.960
I'm the crocodile.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.806
cried the elephant's child.

00:05:51.866 --> 00:05:54.097
What do you eat?

00:05:54.157 --> 00:05:57.148
Come here.

00:05:57.208 --> 00:06:00.420
The crocodile told him again.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.842
The elephant's child came very close to the crocodile.

00:06:05.902 --> 00:06:11.384
The crocodile opened his mouth very wide.

00:06:11.444 --> 00:06:15.386
He held the elephant's child by the nose.

00:06:15.446 --> 00:06:26.958
Today, said the crocodile, I think I'll eat an elephant's child.

00:06:27.018 --> 00:06:31.192
Part 3.

00:06:31.251 --> 00:06:35.796
The elephant's child and the crocodile.

00:06:35.856 --> 00:06:40.410
The python snake arrived at that moment.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:46.178
He said to the elephant's child, The crocodile will eat you, my friend.

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:49.050
You must pull as hard as you can.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.522
Pull, my young friend.

00:06:51.582 --> 00:06:52.793
Pull!"

00:06:52.853 --> 00:06:57.607
The elephant's child pulled and he pulled.

00:06:57.666 --> 00:06:59.538
It was no good.

00:06:59.597 --> 00:07:03.730
His nose was still in the crocodile's mouth.

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:08.464
The crocodile also pulled as hard as he could.

00:07:08.524 --> 00:07:12.200
He pulled and he pulled.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:19.138
The elephant's child's nose began to grow longer and longer.

00:07:19.198 --> 00:07:27.162
Suddenly the elephant's child gave an extra hard pull and he was free.

00:07:27.222 --> 00:07:30.554
He sat back on the ground.

00:07:30.614 --> 00:07:36.338
His nose was very long now and very warm.

00:07:36.398 --> 00:07:43.541
He put it into the great, grey, green Limpopo river for a moment.

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:45.952
Why are you doing that?

00:07:46.012 --> 00:07:48.443
asked the python snake.

00:07:48.503 --> 00:07:51.004
My nose is too long.

00:07:51.064 --> 00:07:53.244
The elephant's child told him.

00:07:53.304 --> 00:07:57.306
Perhaps the river will make it small again.

00:07:57.366 --> 00:08:02.098
Your nose will always be like that now.

00:08:02.157 --> 00:08:04.108
The python told him.

00:08:04.168 --> 00:08:06.315
But I don't like it.

00:08:06.375 --> 00:08:08.532
cried the elephant's child.

00:08:08.592 --> 00:08:13.296
Perhaps you'll like it one day, the python said.

00:08:13.355 --> 00:08:16.948
But it isn't a nose anymore.

00:08:17.008 --> 00:08:20.190
It's too long for a nose.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.383
We'll call it a trunk.

00:08:23.443 --> 00:08:28.126
The elephant's child was not very happy at all.

00:08:28.186 --> 00:08:33.815
At that moment, a mosquito flew onto the elephant's child's head.

00:08:33.874 --> 00:08:39.644
The elephant's child lifted his trunk and hit the mosquito.

00:08:39.703 --> 00:08:42.606
The mosquito flew away.

00:08:42.665 --> 00:08:46.008
A trunk is a good thing.

00:08:46.068 --> 00:08:47.780
The python said.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.812
It's very useful.

00:08:50.872 --> 00:08:56.256
The elephant's child began to play with his new trunk.

00:08:56.316 --> 00:08:58.908
He picked up some grass.

00:08:58.968 --> 00:09:01.926
He ate the grass happily.

00:09:01.986 --> 00:09:05.464
Now, tell me something.

00:09:05.524 --> 00:09:07.244
Said the python.

00:09:07.304 --> 00:09:11.305
Do people hit and punish you?

00:09:11.365 --> 00:09:14.356
Do you like that?

00:09:14.416 --> 00:09:16.336
Of course not.

00:09:16.396 --> 00:09:18.487
Replied the elephant's child.

00:09:18.547 --> 00:09:21.098
I don't like it at all.

00:09:21.158 --> 00:09:24.118
The python smiled at him.

00:09:24.178 --> 00:09:26.749
Now you've got a trunk.

00:09:26.809 --> 00:09:28.139
He said.

00:09:28.199 --> 00:09:32.062
Now you can hit and punish them.

00:09:32.122 --> 00:09:35.896
The elephant's child smiled too.

00:09:35.956 --> 00:09:38.627
That's an idea.

00:09:38.688 --> 00:09:40.588
He said slowly.

00:09:40.648 --> 00:09:44.729
A very good idea.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:47.181
He walked home.

00:09:47.241 --> 00:09:52.923
When he arrived, all his uncles and aunts were happy to see him.

00:09:52.983 --> 00:09:56.754
Come here, elephant's child!

00:09:56.814 --> 00:09:58.420
They cried.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.105
The elephant's child moved towards them.

00:10:02.165 --> 00:10:04.626
He smiled happily.

00:10:04.686 --> 00:10:12.219
One by one, he punished his uncles and his aunts with his new trunk.

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:15.920
Where did you get the new nose?

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:17.950
They asked him.

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:20.362
It's not a nose.

00:10:20.422 --> 00:10:23.022
The elephant's child told them.

00:10:23.082 --> 00:10:24.993
It's a trunk.

00:10:25.053 --> 00:10:28.294
I asked the crocodile what he eats.

00:10:28.354 --> 00:10:30.924
Then he gave me the trunk.

00:10:30.984 --> 00:10:34.575
We want trunks too!

00:10:34.635 --> 00:10:37.366
All the elephants cried.

00:10:37.426 --> 00:10:43.437
They ran off to the great, grey, green Limpopo River.

00:10:43.497 --> 00:10:51.169
When they found the crocodile, they asked him, What do you eat?

00:10:51.229 --> 00:10:55.366
The crocodile pulled their noses.

00:10:55.426 --> 00:11:02.749
And now all elephants have got trunks.

00:11:02.809 --> 00:11:13.498
Pigs Is Pigs by Ellis Parker Butler. Part One: The Correct Price for Pigs.

00:11:13.558 --> 00:11:18.954
Mr. Flannery worked for the Interurban Express Company.

00:11:19.014 --> 00:11:23.167
The company delivered parcels all over America.

00:11:23.227 --> 00:11:26.952
This morning Mr. Flannery was very angry.

00:11:27.012 --> 00:11:31.246
His customer, Mr. Morehouse, was also very angry.

00:11:31.306 --> 00:11:33.170
You decide!

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:35.212
shouted Mr. Flannery.

00:11:35.272 --> 00:11:38.276
He indicated the box in front of him.

00:11:38.336 --> 00:11:41.478
The box contained two small guinea pigs.

00:11:41.538 --> 00:11:43.987
They were happy to eat their food.

00:11:44.047 --> 00:11:47.720
You can pay for them and take them away.

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:50.030
Or you can leave them here.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:54.994
But rules are rules, Mr. Morehouse.

00:11:55.054 --> 00:11:59.416
You're an idiot, shouted Mr. Morehouse.

00:11:59.476 --> 00:12:02.308
The company rules are very clear.

00:12:02.368 --> 00:12:03.748
Look!

00:12:03.808 --> 00:12:08.311
Mr. Morehouse indicated a book on the counter.

00:12:08.371 --> 00:12:10.690
Then he read aloud.

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:18.252
Domestic pets in boxes, Franklin to Westcote, twenty-five cents each.

00:12:18.312 --> 00:12:24.676
These are domestic pets, they're in boxes, and they came from Franklin to Westcote.

00:12:24.736 --> 00:12:27.589
I'll pay twenty-five cents each.

00:12:27.649 --> 00:12:29.210
There are two of them.

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:32.272
I'll pay you fifty cents."

00:12:32.332 --> 00:12:35.725
Mr. Flannery picked up the rulebook.

00:12:35.785 --> 00:12:38.756
Now he began to read what it said.

00:12:38.816 --> 00:12:41.816
I know they're domestic pets.

00:12:41.876 --> 00:12:44.318
Mr. Flannery said angrily.

00:12:44.377 --> 00:12:49.491
And the price for domestic pets is 25 cents.

00:12:49.551 --> 00:12:51.892
But they're also pigs.

00:12:51.952 --> 00:12:53.834
I know that too.

00:12:53.894 --> 00:12:58.117
And the price for pigs is 30 cents each.

00:12:58.177 --> 00:13:02.310
You'll have to pay 30 cents each.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:06.332
That's 60 cents for two.

00:13:06.392 --> 00:13:08.424
That's ridiculous.

00:13:08.484 --> 00:13:10.538
replied Mr. Morehouse.

00:13:10.598 --> 00:13:13.382
That rule means ordinary pigs.

00:13:13.442 --> 00:13:14.974
Not guinea pigs.

00:13:15.034 --> 00:13:20.002
It's obvious the correct rate is twenty-five cents each.

00:13:20.062 --> 00:13:23.386
Mr. Flannery was very stubborn.

00:13:23.446 --> 00:13:27.349
pigs are pigs," he said.

00:13:27.409 --> 00:13:30.200
The company doesn't care.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:32.441
They are all pigs.

00:13:32.501 --> 00:13:39.785
Pigs are not domestic animals, and you must pay the rate for pigs.

00:13:39.845 --> 00:13:43.426
Mr. Morehouse was silent for a moment.

00:13:43.486 --> 00:13:46.748
Very well, he said at last.

00:13:46.808 --> 00:13:50.276
I offered you fifty cents and you refused.

00:13:50.336 --> 00:13:55.074
I'll write to the president of the company and tell him everything.

00:13:55.134 --> 00:13:59.968
Keep the pigs, but you must look after them very well.

00:14:00.028 --> 00:14:02.640
Nothing must happen to them."

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:05.561
Mr. Morehouse left the office.

00:14:05.621 --> 00:14:09.744
Mr. Flannery opened the box with the guinea pigs inside.

00:14:09.804 --> 00:14:11.315
He looked at them.

00:14:11.374 --> 00:14:12.795
They were happy.

00:14:12.855 --> 00:14:15.036
Mr. Flannery was happy.

00:14:15.096 --> 00:14:19.598
He knew the rules about pigs.

00:14:19.658 --> 00:14:31.722
Part 2 Letters About Pigs Mr. Morehouse was very angry when he arrived home.

00:14:31.782 --> 00:14:35.764
He wrote a long letter to the president of the company.

00:14:35.824 --> 00:14:40.026
He explained the problem about the rate for the guinea pigs.

00:14:40.086 --> 00:14:47.598
About a week later Mr. Morehouse received a letter from the Interurban Express Company.

00:14:47.658 --> 00:14:50.300
Interurban Express Company.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:54.265
Dear Sir, Please write to the claims department.

00:14:54.325 --> 00:14:56.186
Yours faithfully.

00:14:56.246 --> 00:15:00.988
Mr. Morehouse now wrote to the claims department of the company.

00:15:01.048 --> 00:15:08.122
A few weeks later he received a reply from the claims department of the Interurban Express Company.

00:15:08.182 --> 00:15:10.444
Interurban Express Company.

00:15:10.504 --> 00:15:14.656
Dear sir, the pigs are in Mr. Flannery's office.

00:15:14.716 --> 00:15:17.349
You did not pay anything to the company.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:24.094
You must write to the tariff department about the correct rate for the delivery of the pigs.

00:15:24.154 --> 00:15:26.384
Yours faithfully."

00:15:26.444 --> 00:15:33.047
Mr. Morehouse now wrote to the tariff department of the Interurban Express Company.

00:15:33.107 --> 00:15:43.438
The manager of the tariff department, Mr. Morgan, told his secretary to write to Mr. Flannery, He didn't pay the domestic rate.

00:15:43.498 --> 00:15:45.218
Ask him to explain.

00:15:45.278 --> 00:15:50.159
He ordered and find out the present condition of the animals.

00:15:50.219 --> 00:15:54.690
Mr. Flannery received the letter from the Tariff Department.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:57.070
Present condition of the animals?

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.170
He read.

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:00.881
What does that mean?

00:16:00.941 --> 00:16:02.811
I'll look at them.

00:16:02.871 --> 00:16:05.512
He walked to the back of the office.

00:16:05.572 --> 00:16:07.378
There was a cage there.

00:16:07.438 --> 00:16:09.826
Mr. Flannery looked inside.

00:16:09.886 --> 00:16:12.648
He began to count carefully.

00:16:12.708 --> 00:16:24.368
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight.

00:16:24.428 --> 00:16:25.620
He counted.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.022
And all of them very well.

00:16:29.082 --> 00:16:35.670
Mr. Flannery went back into his office and wrote to the tariff department of the company.

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:43.346
Dear sir, pigs are pigs.

00:16:43.406 --> 00:16:49.430
There are now eight pigs.

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:54.214
They are all well.

00:16:54.274 --> 00:16:58.435
They eat a lot.

00:16:58.496 --> 00:17:03.629
I paid two dollars for their food.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:09.244
Will Mr. Morehouse pay for their food?

00:17:09.304 --> 00:17:12.206
Yours faithfully."

00:17:12.266 --> 00:17:17.719
Mr. Morgan, the head of the Tariff Department, read Mr. Flannery's letter.

00:17:17.779 --> 00:17:19.440
He was very serious.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:23.403
"'Flannery is right," he told his secretary.

00:17:23.423 --> 00:17:26.525
"'Pigs are pigs."

00:17:26.585 --> 00:17:30.198
Then he wrote another letter to Mr. Flannery.

00:17:30.258 --> 00:17:33.548
Interurban Express Company.

00:17:33.608 --> 00:17:38.800
Dear Mr. Flannery, the rules are clear.

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:44.302
When the animals eat food, the customer must pay for it.

00:17:44.362 --> 00:17:50.756
Collect the two dollars from Mr. Morehouse, Yours sincerely Morgan.

00:17:50.816 --> 00:17:54.128
Collect it, Mr. Flannery thought.

00:17:54.188 --> 00:17:56.559
I don't know how to do that.

00:17:56.620 --> 00:18:01.244
Suddenly he had an idea.

00:18:01.304 --> 00:18:08.371
Part three, pigs will never be pigs again.

00:18:08.431 --> 00:18:13.144
Mr. Flannery drove to Mr. Morehouse's house.

00:18:13.204 --> 00:18:17.226
Mr. Morehouse smiled when he came out of the house.

00:18:17.286 --> 00:18:20.698
So you've brought the guinea pigs at last?

00:18:20.758 --> 00:18:25.341
He said, no sir, replied Mr. Flannery.

00:18:25.401 --> 00:18:29.804
I've got a bill for two dollars for their food.

00:18:29.864 --> 00:18:31.642
Will you pay it?

00:18:31.702 --> 00:18:32.991
Two dollars.

00:18:33.051 --> 00:18:35.012
Explained, Mr. Morehouse.

00:18:35.072 --> 00:18:38.685
For food for two guinea pigs, that's ridiculous.

00:18:38.745 --> 00:18:40.837
I certainly won't pay it.

00:18:40.897 --> 00:18:44.940
There aren't two pigs, Mr. Flannery told him.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.482
There are eight of them now.

00:18:47.542 --> 00:18:51.786
Mr. Morehouse closed the door of the house loudly.

00:18:51.846 --> 00:18:55.001
Mr. Flannery drove back to his office.

00:18:55.062 --> 00:19:00.428
Mr. Flannery wrote again to Mr. Morgan the head of the tariff department.

00:19:00.488 --> 00:19:07.022
This time Mr. Morgan spoke to the president of the Interurban Express Company.

00:19:07.082 --> 00:19:11.716
Are they domestic pets or are they pigs?

00:19:11.776 --> 00:19:14.308
Mr. Morgan wanted to know.

00:19:14.368 --> 00:19:20.796
The president thought for a moment, then he said, I don't know.

00:19:20.856 --> 00:19:24.534
I'll write to Professor Gordon.

00:19:24.594 --> 00:19:26.286
He's an expert.

00:19:26.346 --> 00:19:28.238
He'll know.

00:19:28.298 --> 00:19:32.981
Unfortunately, Professor Gordon was in South America.

00:19:33.042 --> 00:19:39.758
The letter from the president of the Interurban Express Company arrived after many months.

00:19:39.818 --> 00:19:42.850
The president forgot about the problem.

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:45.822
Mr. Morgan forgot about the problem.

00:19:45.883 --> 00:19:49.728
Even Mr. Morehouse forgot about the problem.

00:19:49.788 --> 00:19:54.562
Mr. Flannery, however, did not forget about the problem.

00:19:54.622 --> 00:19:57.744
He wrote again to Mr. Morgan.

00:19:57.804 --> 00:20:05.868
Dear Mr. Morgan, what about these pigs?

00:20:05.928 --> 00:20:10.822
There are now 32 of them.

00:20:10.882 --> 00:20:14.364
They still eat a lot.

00:20:14.424 --> 00:20:19.254
My office is now like a zoo.

00:20:19.314 --> 00:20:23.724
Shall I sell the pigs?

00:20:23.784 --> 00:20:26.858
You're sincerely flannery.

00:20:26.918 --> 00:20:30.391
Mr. Morgan wrote back immediately.

00:20:30.451 --> 00:20:36.196
Dear Mr. Flannery, do not sell the pigs.

00:20:36.256 --> 00:20:38.588
They belong to Mr. Morehouse.

00:20:38.648 --> 00:20:42.261
The company must continue to look after them.

00:20:42.322 --> 00:20:45.698
You're sincerely Morgan.

00:20:45.758 --> 00:20:51.506
Mr. Flannery built a special place for the guinea pigs outside the office.

00:20:51.566 --> 00:20:55.710
A few months later he wrote to Mr. Morgan again.

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:58.152
His letter was very short.

00:20:58.212 --> 00:21:01.196
Dear Mr. Morgan!

00:21:01.256 --> 00:21:04.349
160 pigs!

00:21:04.409 --> 00:21:07.464
You're sincerely Flannery!

00:21:07.524 --> 00:21:11.256
At last a letter came from Professor Gordon.

00:21:11.316 --> 00:21:15.117
He explained everything to the president of the company.

00:21:15.177 --> 00:21:17.527
Guinea pigs are not pigs.

00:21:17.587 --> 00:21:21.948
They belong to a completely different family and species.

00:21:22.008 --> 00:21:25.789
Then the twenty-five cent rate is the correct one.

00:21:25.849 --> 00:21:28.440
The president of the company decided.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.820
Mr. Morgan now wrote to Mr. Flannery.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:39.464
Dear Mr. Flannery, Please deliver the 160 guinea pigs to Mr. Morehouse.

00:21:39.524 --> 00:21:44.198
He must pay 25 cents for each guinea pig.

00:21:44.258 --> 00:21:47.410
Yours sincerely, Morgan.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:51.524
Mr. Flannery wrote back immediately to Mr. Morgan.

00:21:51.584 --> 00:21:54.507
Dear Mr. Morgan, there are not 160 pigs.

00:21:54.567 --> 00:21:59.257
There are now 800.

00:21:59.317 --> 00:22:07.009
Do I have to charge Mr. Morehouse for 160 or for 800?

00:22:07.069 --> 00:22:09.810
You're sincerely, Flannery."

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:15.065
The interurban express company considered the problem very carefully.

00:22:15.125 --> 00:22:20.028
This took time, and the number of guinea pigs increased every day.

00:22:20.088 --> 00:22:25.032
Now Mr. Flannery counted 4,064 of them.

00:22:25.092 --> 00:22:29.411
At last Mr. Flannery received a letter from the company.

00:22:29.471 --> 00:22:42.021
Interurban Express Company, dear Mr. Flannery, collect for two guinea pigs fifty cents, deliver them all to Mr. Morehouse, Yours sincerely, Morgan.

00:22:42.081 --> 00:22:47.263
Once again, Mr. Flannery drove to Mr. Morehouse's home.

00:22:47.323 --> 00:22:50.494
Mr. Morehouse did not live there anymore.

00:22:50.554 --> 00:22:53.880
Mr. Flannery asked the company for orders.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:23:00.690
The company told him to send the guinea pigs to the head office of the Interurban Express Company.

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:05.844
Mr. Flannery began to send the cages of guinea pigs to the company.

00:23:05.904 --> 00:23:11.618
The company received cages of guinea pigs for days and days.

00:23:11.678 --> 00:23:16.792
Mr. Flannery sent 280 cages in one week.

00:23:16.852 --> 00:23:20.630
The company now sent him a telegram.

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:23.506
Interurban Express Company.

00:23:23.567 --> 00:23:26.078
Stop sending guinea pigs.

00:23:26.138 --> 00:23:28.549
Warehouse full.

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:32.442
And Mr. Flannery sent another telegram back.

00:23:32.502 --> 00:23:35.314
Can't stop Flannery.

00:23:35.374 --> 00:23:39.728
The company sent an inspector to Mr. Flannery's office.

00:23:39.788 --> 00:23:43.144
The company's wagon was full of guinea pigs.

00:23:43.204 --> 00:23:48.072
That's the last of them, shouted Mr. Flannery angrily.

00:23:48.132 --> 00:23:53.716
I'll know what to do with animals next time, he said.

00:23:53.776 --> 00:23:55.807
Forget the rules.

00:23:55.867 --> 00:23:57.498
That's what I'll do.

00:23:57.558 --> 00:23:59.690
Pigs are pets.

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:01.931
Cows are pets.

00:24:01.991 --> 00:24:04.213
Horses are pets.

00:24:04.273 --> 00:24:07.686
And lions and tigers are pets.

00:24:07.746 --> 00:24:10.678
Everything goes at 25 cents.

00:24:10.738 --> 00:24:15.061
The wagon drove away with the last guinea pigs.

00:24:15.121 --> 00:24:19.604
There is one good thing, Mr. Flannery said happily.

00:24:19.664 --> 00:24:25.018
At least those pigs weren't elephants.

00:24:25.078 --> 00:24:29.790
Listening activity.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:35.784
You will hear a dialogue between John and his mother.

00:24:35.844 --> 00:24:38.036
John?

00:24:38.096 --> 00:24:41.066
Please bring me my glasses.

00:24:41.126 --> 00:24:43.286
Here you are, Mum.

00:24:43.346 --> 00:24:47.500
John, did you take the dog out this morning?

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:51.004
Yes, I took it out before lunch.

00:24:51.064 --> 00:24:53.686
But the rubbish is still here, John.

00:24:53.746 --> 00:24:56.378
When are you going to take it away?

00:24:56.438 --> 00:24:57.970
Okay, Mum.

00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:02.414
I'll bring you a nice cup of tea and then take it out.

00:25:02.474 --> 00:25:07.165
Oh, John, would you bring me some biscuits with my tea?

00:25:07.225 --> 00:25:09.133
All right, mum.

00:25:09.193 --> 00:25:14.926
And when you go out with the rubbish, don't forget to take the clothes to the laundry.

00:25:14.986 --> 00:25:19.768
And while you're at it, remember to take those films to be developed.

00:25:19.828 --> 00:25:22.610
John, are you listening?

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:24.120
John?

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:25.892
John?

00:25:25.952 --> 00:25:30.144
Are you listening to me?

00:25:30.204 --> 00:25:37.298
Mrs. Packletide's Tiger by Saki.

00:25:37.358 --> 00:25:39.490
Part 1.

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:43.573
Mrs. Packletide shoots a tiger.

00:25:43.633 --> 00:25:47.336
Mrs. Packletide was in India.

00:25:47.396 --> 00:25:50.508
She wanted to kill a tiger.

00:25:50.567 --> 00:25:55.942
She wanted to shoot a tiger for a special reason.

00:25:56.002 --> 00:26:01.106
A friend, Loona Bimberton, flew in a plane.

00:26:01.166 --> 00:26:05.378
Luna talked about the plane all the time.

00:26:05.438 --> 00:26:10.200
Mrs. Packletide was jealous of her friend's adventure.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:13.763
She wanted an adventure as well.

00:26:13.823 --> 00:26:17.286
Mrs. Packletide had a plan.

00:26:17.346 --> 00:26:24.150
She wanted some journalists to take photographs of her with the dead tiger.

00:26:24.211 --> 00:26:32.956
Then she wanted to invite Loona Bimberton to lunch and to give her a Tiger Claw brooch.

00:26:33.016 --> 00:26:37.966
She wanted her friend to be jealous.

00:26:38.026 --> 00:26:47.028
Mrs. Packletide offered the Indians in the village one thousand rupees for a tiger.

00:26:47.088 --> 00:26:53.039
The Indians knew about a very old tiger in the area.

00:26:53.099 --> 00:26:56.570
It was not a dangerous animal.

00:26:56.630 --> 00:27:01.236
Mrs. Packletide was very happy.

00:27:01.296 --> 00:27:10.504
The Indians prepared a platform high up in a tree for Mrs. Packletide and her companion, Miss Mebbin.

00:27:10.564 --> 00:27:15.476
The two ladies climbed onto the platform one night.

00:27:15.536 --> 00:27:20.798
The Indians put a goat on the ground in front of the tree.

00:27:20.858 --> 00:27:24.528
Tigers like to eat goats.

00:27:24.588 --> 00:27:30.992
Mrs. Packletide and Miss Mebbin waited for the tiger to come.

00:27:31.052 --> 00:27:33.866
Is this really dangerous?

00:27:33.926 --> 00:27:35.588
Miss Mebbin asked.

00:27:35.648 --> 00:27:36.929
Not at all.

00:27:36.989 --> 00:27:39.111
Mrs. Packletide told her.

00:27:39.171 --> 00:27:41.513
The tiger is very old.

00:27:41.573 --> 00:27:44.255
He can't jump up into the tree.

00:27:44.315 --> 00:27:46.377
We're safe here.

00:27:46.437 --> 00:27:49.990
Why are you paying 1000 rupees then?

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:52.082
Miss Mebbin wanted to know.

00:27:52.142 --> 00:27:53.883
The tiger's old.

00:27:53.943 --> 00:27:56.846
It's not worth 1000 rupees.

00:27:56.906 --> 00:28:01.031
Miss Mebbin was always interested in money.

00:28:01.092 --> 00:28:03.696
She liked to save money.

00:28:03.756 --> 00:28:07.559
It gave her pleasure to save money.

00:28:07.619 --> 00:28:12.202
Suddenly, the tiger moved out of the darkness.

00:28:12.262 --> 00:28:15.234
It saw the goat on the ground.

00:28:15.294 --> 00:28:21.518
It came very close, and then it lay down.

00:28:21.578 --> 00:28:24.080
It seemed tired.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:29.048
It looks ill, Miss Mebbin said very loudly.

00:28:29.108 --> 00:28:30.994
Be quiet!

00:28:31.054 --> 00:28:33.876
Mrs. Packletide commanded.

00:28:33.936 --> 00:28:39.940
The tiger now got up and began to walk towards the goat again.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:41.731
Shoot now!

00:28:41.792 --> 00:28:43.582
Miss Mebbin suggested.

00:28:43.642 --> 00:28:46.224
Don't let it go near the goat.

00:28:46.284 --> 00:28:49.066
Then we won't have to pay for the goat.

00:28:49.126 --> 00:28:51.478
We can save some money.

00:28:51.538 --> 00:28:55.180
Mrs. Packletide picked up her rifle.

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:58.042
She fired quickly.

00:28:58.102 --> 00:29:02.326
The noise of the rifle was very loud.

00:29:02.386 --> 00:29:07.132
The tiger jumped into the air and then fell to the ground.

00:29:07.192 --> 00:29:09.314
It did not move.

00:29:09.374 --> 00:29:12.486
The Indians were very excited.

00:29:12.546 --> 00:29:15.150
They immediately ran forward.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:18.142
They looked at the dead tiger.

00:29:18.202 --> 00:29:21.544
They were very pleased.

00:29:21.604 --> 00:29:25.544
Mrs. Packletide was pleased, too.

00:29:25.604 --> 00:29:29.784
She thought about her friend, Loona Bimberton.

00:29:29.845 --> 00:29:32.535
Something is wrong.

00:29:32.595 --> 00:29:36.576
Louisa Mebbin said to Mrs. Packletide.

00:29:36.636 --> 00:29:39.167
You shot the goat.

00:29:39.227 --> 00:29:42.258
She told Mrs. Packletide.

00:29:42.318 --> 00:29:45.218
She indicated the goat.

00:29:45.278 --> 00:29:47.118
It was true.

00:29:47.178 --> 00:29:50.048
The goat had a bullet wound.

00:29:50.108 --> 00:29:56.570
There was no bullet wound on the tiger, but it too was dead.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:30:01.041
The old tiger was dead from a heart attack.

00:30:01.102 --> 00:30:05.274
At first Mrs. Packletide was angry.

00:30:05.334 --> 00:30:08.004
Then she thought about it.

00:30:08.064 --> 00:30:10.926
She still had a dead tiger.

00:30:10.986 --> 00:30:16.248
The Indians had their thousand rupees.

00:30:16.308 --> 00:30:20.424
Part two.

00:30:20.484 --> 00:30:24.451
triumph and disaster.

00:30:24.511 --> 00:30:28.152
Soon the journalists came.

00:30:28.212 --> 00:30:32.274
They took photographs of Mrs. Packletide and the Tiger.

00:30:32.334 --> 00:30:39.306
The pictures appeared in many newspapers, even the Texas Weekly Snapshot.

00:30:39.366 --> 00:30:42.297
Loona Bimberton saw them.

00:30:42.357 --> 00:30:45.308
She was very jealous.

00:30:45.368 --> 00:30:49.178
She received the tiger-claw brooch.

00:30:49.238 --> 00:30:51.778
Then she was angry.

00:30:51.838 --> 00:30:57.181
She did not accept Mrs. Packletide's invitation to lunch.

00:30:57.241 --> 00:31:02.835
Mrs. Packletide and Miss Mebbin returned to London.

00:31:02.895 --> 00:31:06.238
They brought the tiger skin with them.

00:31:06.298 --> 00:31:08.960
Everyone admired it.

00:31:09.019 --> 00:31:15.244
Mrs. Packletide was very pleased and satisfied with her adventure.

00:31:15.304 --> 00:31:18.596
She went to a fancy dress party.

00:31:18.656 --> 00:31:21.888
She wore a Diana costume.

00:31:21.948 --> 00:31:24.329
No one knows the truth.

00:31:24.389 --> 00:31:27.580
Miss Mebbin said a few days later.

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:29.442
What do you mean?

00:31:29.502 --> 00:31:32.824
Mrs. Packletide asked angrily.

00:31:32.884 --> 00:31:35.745
You didn't shoot the tiger at all.

00:31:35.805 --> 00:31:37.556
Miss Mebbin explained.

00:31:37.616 --> 00:31:39.287
You shot the goat.

00:31:39.347 --> 00:31:42.669
The tiger just died because it was afraid.

00:31:42.729 --> 00:31:45.832
Miss Mebbin laughed.

00:31:45.892 --> 00:31:50.295
Mrs. Packletide's face went red.

00:31:50.355 --> 00:31:52.446
No one will believe that.

00:31:52.506 --> 00:31:54.218
She said quickly.

00:31:54.278 --> 00:31:57.089
Loona Bimberton will believe it.

00:31:57.149 --> 00:32:00.120
Miss Mebbin told her quietly.

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:03.832
Mrs. Packletide went very white.

00:32:03.893 --> 00:32:06.594
Don't tell anyone.

00:32:06.654 --> 00:32:08.846
She said quietly.

00:32:08.906 --> 00:32:11.817
There's a little cottage near Dorking.

00:32:11.877 --> 00:32:14.538
Miss Mebbin replied slowly.

00:32:14.598 --> 00:32:17.357
I want to buy it.

00:32:17.418 --> 00:32:25.576
It costs £680, but I haven't got the money.

00:32:25.636 --> 00:32:31.308
Louisa Mebbin has her little cottage in the country now.

00:32:31.368 --> 00:32:34.428
It is a very pretty place.

00:32:34.488 --> 00:32:38.169
All her friends like it very much.

00:32:38.229 --> 00:32:41.820
How does she pay to keep all this?

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:43.998
They all ask.

00:32:44.058 --> 00:32:49.049
Mrs. Packletide does not hunt tigers anymore.

00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:51.521
It's too expensive.

00:32:51.581 --> 00:32:55.322
She tells her friends.

00:32:55.382 --> 00:32:59.585
Listening activity.

00:32:59.645 --> 00:33:07.049
You will hear an interview with Mrs. Packletide about how she shot the tiger.

00:33:07.109 --> 00:33:09.130
Good evening.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:14.813
This is Leonard Burner reporting about an extraordinary trophy.

00:33:14.873 --> 00:33:23.736
The well-to-do Mrs. Packletide is very proud about her catch, a real Indian tiger.

00:33:23.797 --> 00:33:32.070
Today, she met her friend Loona Bimberton, the well-known pilot, and gave her a tiger claw brooch.

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:36.488
Mrs. Packletide, tell us about your adventure.

00:33:36.548 --> 00:33:42.737
Yes, well, good evening to all the lovely listeners out there.

00:33:42.797 --> 00:33:44.228
What can I say?

00:33:44.248 --> 00:33:52.740
I went to India, in the middle of the jungle, my goodness, with all those beastly animals.

00:33:52.800 --> 00:34:00.124
You understand, it's thick jungle, where you see nothing but wild animals.

00:34:00.184 --> 00:34:02.994
Yes, yes, very dangerous.

00:34:03.054 --> 00:34:04.614
Did you go alone?

00:34:04.674 --> 00:34:07.213
Well, not exactly.

00:34:07.273 --> 00:34:13.346
You see, Miss Louisa Mebbin, my dear companion, would not let me go alone.

00:34:13.406 --> 00:34:19.169
You must understand that I love Miss Mebbin, but she's not a courageous soul.

00:34:19.229 --> 00:34:22.540
I told Miss Mebbin that it was dangerous.

00:34:22.600 --> 00:34:27.463
You mean, Mrs. Packletide, that Miss Mebbin did not help you?

00:34:27.523 --> 00:34:31.504
Well, I don't want to be rude.

00:34:31.564 --> 00:34:36.415
But Miss Mebbin is not the ideal hunting companion.

00:34:36.475 --> 00:34:42.748
Well, Mrs. Packletide, please tell the listeners how you managed to kill the tiger.

00:34:42.808 --> 00:34:53.394
You see, I had this rifle, and well, we were walking in the jungle unprotected.

00:34:53.454 --> 00:34:55.808
Yes, do go on.

00:34:55.868 --> 00:35:03.952
And yes, this big Indian tiger appeared suddenly How frightening.

00:35:04.012 --> 00:35:06.484
Oh yes, it was awful.

00:35:06.544 --> 00:35:09.587
It came running out of the thick jungle.

00:35:09.647 --> 00:35:11.208
My goodness.

00:35:11.268 --> 00:35:14.140
It ran straight towards Miss Mebbin.

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:15.472
Oh no.

00:35:15.532 --> 00:35:16.742
Exactly.

00:35:16.803 --> 00:35:18.864
I had to save her life.

00:35:18.924 --> 00:35:21.927
So you shot and saved Miss Mebbin's life?

00:35:21.987 --> 00:35:26.591
Well, you know, she is my companion.

00:35:26.651 --> 00:35:28.632
Yes, I understand.

00:35:28.692 --> 00:35:31.372
A very brave act, Mrs. Packletide.

00:35:31.432 --> 00:35:33.153
A very brave act.

00:35:33.213 --> 00:35:36.644
Thank goodness you saved her life.

00:35:36.704 --> 00:35:42.406
Yes, thank goodness she's alive.

00:35:42.466 --> 00:35:45.227
Well, thank you.

00:35:45.287 --> 00:35:46.937
Yes, thank you.

00:35:46.997 --> 00:35:49.938
No, thank you, Mrs. Packletide.

00:35:49.998 --> 00:35:54.663
And this is the end of today's broadcaster.

00:35:54.723 --> 00:36:12.882
The Stolen White Elephant by Mark Twain. Part One: A Royal Present. Once I met a man on a train who told me a story. This is his story.

00:36:12.942 --> 00:36:17.883
The White Elephant is a very important animal in Siam.

00:36:17.943 --> 00:36:20.667
Only the king can possess one.

00:36:20.727 --> 00:36:27.236
Many years ago, Britain and Siam argued about the frontier line of Siam.

00:36:27.296 --> 00:36:32.490
Siam was wrong and the two countries soon became friends again.

00:36:32.551 --> 00:36:41.068
The King of Siam decided to send the British Queen a magnificent present, a white elephant.

00:36:41.128 --> 00:36:46.878
It was my job to transport the white elephant from Siam to England.

00:36:46.938 --> 00:36:53.628
We left Siam in a ship with the elephant, and at last we arrived in New York.

00:36:53.688 --> 00:36:58.022
We decided to send the elephant to New Jersey for a while.

00:36:58.082 --> 00:37:02.666
He needed rest after the first part of the long voyage.

00:37:02.726 --> 00:37:06.320
Then a terrible thing suddenly happened.

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:09.102
Someone stole the white elephant.

00:37:09.163 --> 00:37:11.966
I went to the New York police.

00:37:12.026 --> 00:37:16.540
The chief detective was called Inspector Blunt.

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:20.062
He listened to my story in silence.

00:37:20.122 --> 00:37:25.196
This is not an ordinary case, he said.

00:37:25.216 --> 00:37:28.658
Do not talk to the reporters.

00:37:28.718 --> 00:37:31.472
I will manage them.

00:37:31.532 --> 00:37:36.942
Now, he said, what's the elephant's name?

00:37:37.002 --> 00:37:50.167
Hassan, Ben, Ali, Ben, Selim, Abdullah, Muhammad, Moise, Alhamal, Jamster, Jeebohoy, Dhulip, Sultan, Ebubudpur.

00:37:50.227 --> 00:37:51.788
I told him.

00:37:51.848 --> 00:37:53.570
Good.

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:55.591
First name?

00:37:55.651 --> 00:37:57.592
Jumbo.

00:37:57.652 --> 00:38:00.074
Are his parents living?

00:38:00.094 --> 00:38:02.296
No, they're dead.

00:38:02.356 --> 00:38:05.128
Was he the only child?

00:38:05.188 --> 00:38:07.072
Yes, he was.

00:38:07.132 --> 00:38:09.804
Now describe it.

00:38:09.864 --> 00:38:12.775
Inspector Blunt commanded me.

00:38:12.836 --> 00:38:15.996
I began describing the white elephant.

00:38:16.056 --> 00:38:34.182
Height 19 feet, length of trunk 16 feet, length of tail 6 feet, total length including trunk and tail 48 feet, color of the elephant, a dull white.

00:38:34.242 --> 00:38:37.999
He has the habit of spraying people with water.

00:38:38.059 --> 00:38:41.876
He has a small scar under his arm.

00:38:41.936 --> 00:38:44.966
The detective wrote everything down.

00:38:45.027 --> 00:38:47.857
Now I want a photograph!

00:38:47.917 --> 00:38:50.168
Inspector Blunt told me.

00:38:50.228 --> 00:38:53.839
I gave him a photograph of the white elephant.

00:38:53.899 --> 00:38:57.780
Then he rang a bell and a boy came in.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:04.972
Make fifty thousand copies of this description and photograph, Alaric!

00:39:05.032 --> 00:39:07.397
He ordered the boy.

00:39:07.458 --> 00:39:11.776
Give them to all the detective offices.

00:39:11.836 --> 00:39:14.188
Alaric left the room.

00:39:14.248 --> 00:39:17.330
We will have to offer a reward.

00:39:17.390 --> 00:39:19.582
Inspector Blunt told me.

00:39:19.642 --> 00:39:21.033
How much?

00:39:21.093 --> 00:39:22.464
I asked.

00:39:22.524 --> 00:39:27.258
To begin with twenty-five thousand dollars.

00:39:27.318 --> 00:39:29.009
The inspector told me.

00:39:29.069 --> 00:39:34.257
The thieves are very clever and they have friends everywhere.

00:39:34.318 --> 00:39:36.333
Do you know who they are?"

00:39:36.394 --> 00:39:39.656
I asked in great surprise and astonishment.

00:39:39.716 --> 00:39:44.710
We usually have an idea who the criminal is.

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:46.272
He told me.

00:39:46.332 --> 00:39:51.406
Inspector Blunt then asked me about the food that the elephant ate.

00:39:51.466 --> 00:39:52.862
Men.

00:39:52.922 --> 00:39:55.840
Does he eat men?"

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:58.102
The inspector asked.

00:39:58.162 --> 00:40:00.775
Oh, yes, I replied.

00:40:00.835 --> 00:40:03.087
But he will eat anything.

00:40:03.148 --> 00:40:11.355
He will eat books, bottles, clothes, cats, potatoes, rice, anything at all.

00:40:11.416 --> 00:40:14.969
Good, but we need details.

00:40:15.029 --> 00:40:18.437
Details are necessary.

00:40:18.497 --> 00:40:25.414
During one day, how many men will he eat if they are fresh?

00:40:25.475 --> 00:40:29.256
He does not care if they are fresh or not.

00:40:29.316 --> 00:40:32.977
At a single meal he can eat five men.

00:40:33.037 --> 00:40:36.608
What nationality does he prefer?

00:40:36.668 --> 00:40:41.970
He doesn't care about nationalities, but he prefers people he knows.

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:43.831
What does he drink?

00:40:43.891 --> 00:40:46.167
The inspector wanted to know.

00:40:46.227 --> 00:40:49.904
He drinks anything as well, I told him.

00:40:49.964 --> 00:40:53.066
These are unusual facts.

00:40:53.126 --> 00:40:55.026
The inspector commented.

00:40:55.086 --> 00:41:00.820
They will help us to find the elephant.

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:05.282
Listening activity.

00:41:05.342 --> 00:41:12.276
You will hear a police inspector interviewing someone about a missing person.

00:41:12.336 --> 00:41:16.314
This is a strange case.

00:41:16.374 --> 00:41:19.471
What is your husband's name?

00:41:19.531 --> 00:41:23.232
His name is Arnold Landlow.

00:41:23.292 --> 00:41:25.984
Do spell that for me, ma'am.

00:41:26.044 --> 00:41:27.855
Yes, of course.

00:41:27.915 --> 00:41:38.960
L, A, N, D, L, O, W.

00:41:39.020 --> 00:41:40.690
Very good.

00:41:40.750 --> 00:41:43.232
And where does he live?

00:41:43.292 --> 00:41:46.488
Well, he lives with me, of course.

00:41:46.548 --> 00:41:48.904
The address, the address.

00:41:48.964 --> 00:41:52.138
I need to know his address, please, ma'am.

00:41:52.197 --> 00:41:53.918
Yes.

00:41:53.978 --> 00:42:03.788
We live at 105, 3rd Avenue, New York.

00:42:03.848 --> 00:42:06.440
Very good, very good.

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:09.262
And what does he look like?

00:42:09.322 --> 00:42:14.158
Oh, Inspector, dreadful.

00:42:14.218 --> 00:42:17.221
He looks just dreadful.

00:42:17.282 --> 00:42:19.062
Come on, ma'am.

00:42:19.122 --> 00:42:22.894
I mean, can you describe him, please?

00:42:22.954 --> 00:42:26.546
Well, he's short.

00:42:26.606 --> 00:42:28.567
Very short.

00:42:28.627 --> 00:42:30.798
He's thin.

00:42:30.858 --> 00:42:33.299
Very thin.

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:38.472
And well, he doesn't have any hair on his head.

00:42:38.532 --> 00:42:41.462
He's completely bald.

00:42:41.522 --> 00:42:46.712
Oh, but he has hair on the palms of his hands.

00:42:46.772 --> 00:42:50.183
Hair on the palms of his hands?

00:42:50.243 --> 00:42:52.624
You mean he's a beast?

00:42:52.684 --> 00:42:53.895
No, sir.

00:42:53.955 --> 00:42:55.645
Well, sort of.

00:42:55.706 --> 00:42:56.646
No.

00:42:56.706 --> 00:42:58.887
No, he is a man, you know.

00:42:58.947 --> 00:43:00.427
He's very clever.

00:43:00.447 --> 00:43:02.488
Hmm.

00:43:02.548 --> 00:43:07.850
You say you lost him after you went shopping with him.

00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:12.594
Well, Inspector, he lost the keys to the house.

00:43:12.654 --> 00:43:17.077
We went to the department store and made a new set of keys.

00:43:17.137 --> 00:43:18.858
Then what happened?

00:43:18.918 --> 00:43:22.801
Well, he had to go to the barbers for a shave.

00:43:22.861 --> 00:43:24.922
Yes, I see.

00:43:24.982 --> 00:43:27.454
And well, I went home.

00:43:27.514 --> 00:43:31.438
Did you go back to the barbers when he did not come home?

00:43:31.498 --> 00:43:37.936
Well, I left the house almost immediately because I had to visit my sick aunt.

00:43:37.996 --> 00:43:40.482
Did you return home after that?

00:43:40.542 --> 00:43:41.974
Yes, I did.

00:43:42.034 --> 00:43:45.366
I went home and he wasn't there.

00:43:45.426 --> 00:43:47.617
So what did you do then?

00:43:47.677 --> 00:43:51.710
I went to the barbers, but he wasn't there.

00:43:51.770 --> 00:43:54.522
Can I see the keys to your house?

00:43:54.582 --> 00:43:55.882
Of course.

00:43:55.942 --> 00:43:57.560
Here they are.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:44:00.338
two sets of keys.

00:44:00.399 --> 00:44:01.969
Listen here, ma'am.

00:44:02.029 --> 00:44:05.422
An officer will go to your house with you immediately.

00:44:05.482 --> 00:44:09.304
But first I must examine these sets of keys.

00:44:09.364 --> 00:44:12.926
In your statement, you said you only had one set.

00:44:12.986 --> 00:44:17.779
And well, here we are with two sets.

00:44:17.839 --> 00:44:21.681
Part two.

00:44:21.742 --> 00:44:25.542
Inspector Blunt gives his orders.

00:44:25.602 --> 00:44:30.361
Inspector Blunt now called Captain Burns.

00:44:30.421 --> 00:44:35.872
The Inspector described the case to him, and then he gave his instructions.

00:44:35.932 --> 00:44:43.504
Tell Detective Jones, Davis, Bates, and Hackett to follow the elephant.

00:44:43.564 --> 00:44:53.416
And tell Detective Moses, Dacon, Murphy, Rogers, and Bartholomew to follow the thieves.

00:44:53.476 --> 00:44:56.067
Yes, sir, the Captain said.

00:44:56.127 --> 00:44:59.219
We'll find your elephant."

00:44:59.279 --> 00:45:01.151
The inspector told me.

00:45:01.211 --> 00:45:02.622
I thanked him.

00:45:02.682 --> 00:45:07.126
I liked that man, and I liked the way he approached the case.

00:45:07.186 --> 00:45:12.470
The next morning the newspapers were full of the story of the Stolen Elephant.

00:45:12.531 --> 00:45:18.436
Some of the newspapers gave the opinions of different detectives about the case.

00:45:18.496 --> 00:45:21.970
Each detective had a different opinion.

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:27.798
All the detectives named the criminals, but they all gave different names.

00:45:27.858 --> 00:45:33.342
The newspaper articles finished with a mention of Inspector Blunt.

00:45:33.402 --> 00:45:40.058
He knows that the criminals are Brick, Duffy, and Red McFadden.

00:45:40.118 --> 00:45:44.026
He knew that they had plans for the crime.

00:45:44.086 --> 00:45:47.613
I was pleased when I read the newspaper article.

00:45:47.673 --> 00:45:52.726
It was clear that Inspector Blunt was a very clever detective.

00:45:52.786 --> 00:45:56.668
I hurried to his office to talk to him again.

00:45:56.728 --> 00:46:00.880
You know who the thieves are, I said.

00:46:00.940 --> 00:46:04.832
Why didn't you arrest them before the crime?

00:46:04.893 --> 00:46:07.964
We do not prevent crime.

00:46:08.024 --> 00:46:09.315
He told me.

00:46:09.375 --> 00:46:12.896
We punish crime.

00:46:12.956 --> 00:46:17.837
We cannot punish it until it is committed.

00:46:17.897 --> 00:46:24.320
Then I noticed something strange about the reward for twenty-five thousand dollars.

00:46:24.380 --> 00:46:27.672
The reward was only for detectives.

00:46:27.732 --> 00:46:29.693
What about the public?

00:46:29.753 --> 00:46:30.683
I asked.

00:46:30.743 --> 00:46:34.885
The detectives will find the elephant.

00:46:34.945 --> 00:46:36.676
He explained to me.

00:46:36.736 --> 00:46:40.588
That is why the reward is for them.

00:46:40.648 --> 00:46:48.085
Now the telegraph in the office began to bring in the first messages from the inspector's men.

00:46:48.145 --> 00:46:52.739
Flower Station, New York, 7.30 a.m.

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:54.861
Found a clue.

00:46:54.921 --> 00:46:58.514
Think the elephant is going west.

00:46:58.574 --> 00:47:00.626
I am following.

00:47:00.686 --> 00:47:03.488
Darlie, detective.

00:47:03.548 --> 00:47:08.916
Darlie's a good man, the inspector said.

00:47:08.976 --> 00:47:11.609
Another telegram arrived.

00:47:11.669 --> 00:47:15.462
Flower Station, New York, 9 a.m.

00:47:15.522 --> 00:47:18.433
Followed the tracks for three miles.

00:47:18.493 --> 00:47:22.005
A farmer told me they are not elephant tracks.

00:47:22.065 --> 00:47:25.336
They are holes he made for planting trees.

00:47:25.397 --> 00:47:27.278
Tell me what to do.

00:47:27.338 --> 00:47:29.819
Darlie, detective.

00:47:29.879 --> 00:47:33.310
The inspector sent Darlie a telegram.

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:35.574
Arrest the man.

00:47:35.634 --> 00:47:38.987
He is one of the thieves.

00:47:39.047 --> 00:47:41.458
Follow the tracks.

00:47:41.518 --> 00:47:43.590
Inspector Blunt.

00:47:43.650 --> 00:47:47.533
Next, a telegram arrived from Haws.

00:47:47.593 --> 00:47:52.146
Ironville, New York, 9.30 AM.

00:47:52.206 --> 00:47:54.748
The elephant was here this morning.

00:47:54.808 --> 00:47:56.339
No one knows where he went.

00:47:56.399 --> 00:47:58.140
He killed a horse.

00:47:58.201 --> 00:48:01.142
Haws, detective.

00:48:01.202 --> 00:48:04.716
Inspector Blunt called Captain Burns.

00:48:04.776 --> 00:48:09.532
send a lot of men to Ironville," he ordered.

00:48:09.592 --> 00:48:14.816
More messages arrived from all over New Jersey and Pennsylvania.

00:48:14.877 --> 00:48:17.728
Then a very strange one arrived.

00:48:17.788 --> 00:48:20.972
It was from the famous circus Barnum's.

00:48:21.032 --> 00:48:27.768
It offered $4,000 a year to use the elephant for advertising the circus.

00:48:27.828 --> 00:48:32.801
The idea was to attach advertisements to the elephant's body.

00:48:32.861 --> 00:48:35.492
Ridiculous, I cried.

00:48:35.552 --> 00:48:37.403
Of course it is.

00:48:37.463 --> 00:48:39.284
Inspector Blunt agreed.

00:48:39.344 --> 00:48:45.116
Mr. Barnum does not know me, but I know him.

00:48:45.176 --> 00:48:48.678
The Inspector sent a message to Mr. Barnum.

00:48:48.738 --> 00:48:52.959
Seven thousand dollars or nothing.

00:48:53.019 --> 00:48:54.990
Inspector Blunt.

00:48:55.050 --> 00:48:57.910
Back came an immediate reply.

00:48:57.970 --> 00:48:59.340
Agreed.

00:48:59.400 --> 00:49:00.231
P.T.

00:49:00.271 --> 00:49:01.411
Barnum.

00:49:01.472 --> 00:49:05.653
A little while later, other telegrams arrived.

00:49:05.713 --> 00:49:08.574
Glovers, 11.15 a.m.

00:49:08.634 --> 00:49:09.855
Just arrived.

00:49:09.915 --> 00:49:13.236
The village is deserted except for the sick and the old.

00:49:13.297 --> 00:49:19.339
During a village meeting, the elephant put his trunk in a window and washed the meeting room with water.

00:49:19.399 --> 00:49:21.980
Some people drank the water and died.

00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:23.281
Others drowned.

00:49:23.341 --> 00:49:25.812
The whole region lives in terror.

00:49:25.872 --> 00:49:26.862
Brandt.

00:49:26.922 --> 00:49:28.697
Detective.

00:49:28.758 --> 00:49:33.354
Bolivia, New York, 12.50 pm.

00:49:33.414 --> 00:49:37.155
The elephant arrived here and disturbed a funeral procession.

00:49:37.215 --> 00:49:38.866
It killed two people.

00:49:38.926 --> 00:49:41.367
Several citizens tried to shoot it.

00:49:41.427 --> 00:49:43.338
Some of the bullets hit it.

00:49:43.398 --> 00:49:47.519
Detective Burke and I followed the animal into the woods.

00:49:47.579 --> 00:49:52.511
The elephant stopped to rest, and Burke came very close to it.

00:49:52.571 --> 00:49:56.063
He shouted out, the reward is mine.

00:49:56.124 --> 00:49:59.576
The elephant turned round and killed him with its trunk.

00:49:59.636 --> 00:50:00.927
I ran away.

00:50:00.987 --> 00:50:04.429
The elephant chased me back to the funeral procession.

00:50:04.489 --> 00:50:07.161
The elephant killed more people.

00:50:07.221 --> 00:50:09.883
I do not know where it is now.

00:50:09.943 --> 00:50:12.734
Mulroney, detective.

00:50:12.794 --> 00:50:18.454
That was the final telegram that we received.

00:50:18.514 --> 00:50:21.972
Part 3.

00:50:22.032 --> 00:50:27.544
The End of the Case I was very unhappy.

00:50:27.604 --> 00:50:33.696
The elephant was responsible for many deaths, and I was responsible for the elephant.

00:50:33.756 --> 00:50:39.278
There were more telegrams that day from all over America about the elephant.

00:50:39.338 --> 00:50:42.989
But it was clear that no one knew where it was.

00:50:43.049 --> 00:50:45.872
It was the same thing the next day.

00:50:45.932 --> 00:50:49.716
The newspapers were tired with the story now.

00:50:49.776 --> 00:50:52.727
There was nothing exciting to report.

00:50:52.787 --> 00:51:01.111
The inspector suggested increasing the reward from $25,000 to $50,000.

00:51:01.171 --> 00:51:03.512
Four days passed.

00:51:03.572 --> 00:51:06.884
There was still no news of the elephant.

00:51:06.944 --> 00:51:15.042
I followed the inspector's advice again and increased the reward from $50,000 to $75,000.

00:51:15.102 --> 00:51:20.529
The newspapers now began to attack the detectives.

00:51:20.589 --> 00:51:23.420
They made rude comments about them.

00:51:23.480 --> 00:51:28.423
Only one man remained calm, and that was Inspector Blunt.

00:51:28.483 --> 00:51:31.004
I admired him very much.

00:51:31.064 --> 00:51:38.348
About three weeks after the elephant disappeared, Inspector Blunt had a brilliant idea.

00:51:38.408 --> 00:51:44.034
He decided to contact the thieves and make an agreement with them.

00:51:44.094 --> 00:51:49.502
One hundred thousand dollars will be enough, he said.

00:51:49.562 --> 00:51:54.596
We can't give the thieves a hundred thousand dollars, I said.

00:51:54.656 --> 00:51:57.019
The detectives won't get anything.

00:51:57.079 --> 00:51:59.200
Yes, they will.

00:51:59.261 --> 00:52:01.372
Inspector Blunt told me.

00:52:01.432 --> 00:52:06.734
When there is an agreement, the detectives get half of the money.

00:52:06.794 --> 00:52:16.628
Inspector Blunt wrote two letters, one to the wife of Brick Duffy and the other to the wife of Red McFadden.

00:52:16.688 --> 00:52:24.244
Dear Madam, your husband can make a lot of money if he comes to see me.

00:52:24.304 --> 00:52:29.998
Inspector Blunt, he soon received these replies to the letters.

00:52:30.058 --> 00:52:32.320
You old fool!

00:52:32.380 --> 00:52:35.482
Brick McDuffie died two years ago.

00:52:35.542 --> 00:52:40.620
Chief, They hanged Red McFadden 18 months ago.

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:44.136
Inspector Blunt then had another idea.

00:52:44.196 --> 00:52:48.720
He wrote a mysterious advertisement in the morning papers.

00:52:48.780 --> 00:52:56.190
He told me this advertisement invited the thief to meet him at midnight the following night.

00:52:56.250 --> 00:52:59.711
I arrived at the inspector's office at 11 p.m.

00:52:59.751 --> 00:53:03.402
the following night with the $100,000.

00:53:03.462 --> 00:53:06.822
He took the money and he left the office.

00:53:06.882 --> 00:53:08.463
I waited there.

00:53:08.523 --> 00:53:10.764
At last he came back.

00:53:10.824 --> 00:53:13.014
He looked very happy.

00:53:13.074 --> 00:53:15.505
We've made a deal!

00:53:15.565 --> 00:53:17.098
Follow me!

00:53:17.158 --> 00:53:22.502
I followed the detective down into the basement where the detectives worked.

00:53:22.562 --> 00:53:24.154
It was dark there.

00:53:24.214 --> 00:53:27.125
The inspector fell over a large object.

00:53:27.185 --> 00:53:29.596
Then he cried out excitedly.

00:53:29.656 --> 00:53:31.878
The elephant!

00:53:31.938 --> 00:53:33.798
I found it!

00:53:33.859 --> 00:53:36.950
All the detectives were very happy now.

00:53:37.011 --> 00:53:41.332
Everybody congratulated the inspector on his discovery.

00:53:41.392 --> 00:53:45.535
Give that man a clue and he'll find anything!

00:53:45.595 --> 00:53:47.279
They said happily.

00:53:47.340 --> 00:53:53.406
Then they divided the $100,000 and that was a happy occasion too.

00:53:53.466 --> 00:53:58.820
The next day the newspapers were full of praise except for one.

00:53:58.880 --> 00:54:02.322
It said, What a great detective!

00:54:02.382 --> 00:54:07.555
He may be a little slow to find a little thing like a lost elephant.

00:54:07.615 --> 00:54:13.644
He hunted him all day and slept with his dead body all night for three weeks.

00:54:13.704 --> 00:54:20.052
However, he found him at last, because the man who lost him showed him the place.

00:54:20.112 --> 00:54:22.873
Poor Hassan was dead.

00:54:22.933 --> 00:54:25.674
The bullets wounded him fatally.

00:54:25.734 --> 00:54:34.039
He reached the detective's building, and there, surrounded by his enemies, he suffered and died of hunger.

00:54:34.099 --> 00:54:40.493
This adventure cost me $100,000, plus my detective expenses of $42,000.

00:54:40.553 --> 00:54:45.816
I never worked for the government again.

00:54:45.877 --> 00:54:49.890
I do not have any money, and I am a ruined man.

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:57.095
But I admire Inspector Blunt as the greatest detective in the world.

00:54:57.155 --> 00:55:07.152
Listening activity Listen to the conversation between a man and a police officer.

00:55:07.212 --> 00:55:09.210
Hello.

00:55:09.270 --> 00:55:16.422
Is that nine, eight, seven, three, six, five?

00:55:16.482 --> 00:55:18.064
Yes it is.

00:55:18.124 --> 00:55:20.236
How can I help you, sir?

00:55:20.297 --> 00:55:23.128
I'm calling to claim the $45,000 reward.

00:55:23.188 --> 00:55:26.292
The $35,000 reward is for the missing guinea pig.

00:55:26.352 --> 00:55:31.591
Where did you find it?

00:55:31.651 --> 00:55:44.192
No, I'm claiming the $45,000 reward for the missing needle in the haystack.

00:55:44.252 --> 00:55:47.283
Sir, I have good news.

00:55:47.343 --> 00:55:52.305
The missing needle in the haystack reward is now worth $100,000.

00:55:52.365 --> 00:55:56.263
What do you mean good news?

00:55:56.323 --> 00:56:04.962
Do you mean to say it has gone down to ten thousand dollars?

00:56:05.022 --> 00:56:07.082
No, no, sir.

00:56:07.142 --> 00:56:11.064
It has gone up to one hundred thousand.

00:56:11.124 --> 00:56:16.355
One zero zero zero zero zero dollars.

00:56:16.415 --> 00:56:18.596
Really?

00:56:18.656 --> 00:56:22.304
Tell me what do I have to do?

00:56:22.364 --> 00:56:29.275
Well, all you have to do is tell me where you found the needle, sir, and the money is yours.

00:56:29.335 --> 00:56:32.306
Why, that's easy!

00:56:32.366 --> 00:56:36.298
I found it in a haystack, of course!

00:56:36.358 --> 00:56:40.230
How about that?

00:56:40.290 --> 00:56:51.225
The Shameful Behaviour of a Fox Terrier, from Three Men in a Boat by Jerome K. Jerome.

00:56:51.245 --> 00:57:04.254
Part One: The Haymarket Stores. Fox terriers are about four times more mischievous than other dogs.

00:57:04.314 --> 00:57:07.726
Their behaviour is shameful.

00:57:07.786 --> 00:57:13.158
It takes years and years of patience to train them.

00:57:13.218 --> 00:57:18.686
I remember what happened in the lobby of the Haymarket Stores.

00:57:18.746 --> 00:57:23.542
I was in the lobby and there were many dogs all around me.

00:57:23.602 --> 00:57:28.216
Their owners were inside the shop, doing their shopping.

00:57:28.276 --> 00:57:37.494
There was a bulldog, two collies, a St Bernard, some Yorkshire Terriers, a hound and a French poodle.

00:57:37.554 --> 00:57:41.126
There were also a few other smaller dogs.

00:57:41.186 --> 00:57:44.690
They were about as big as rats.

00:57:44.750 --> 00:57:49.214
They all sat in the lobby patiently and silently.

00:57:49.274 --> 00:57:53.817
Then a lovely young lady entered the lobby.

00:57:53.877 --> 00:57:57.209
She had a sweet little fox terrier.

00:57:57.269 --> 00:58:01.851
She left him there between the bulldog and the poodle.

00:58:01.912 --> 00:58:05.944
At first he sat and looked around.

00:58:06.004 --> 00:58:13.298
Then he looked up and started looking at the other dogs who were silent and dignified.

00:58:13.358 --> 00:58:18.404
First he looked at the bulldog who was asleep on his right.

00:58:18.464 --> 00:58:22.771
after he looked at the poodle who was on his left.

00:58:22.831 --> 00:58:28.116
Then suddenly the little fox terrier bit the poodle's front leg.

00:58:28.176 --> 00:58:31.268
There was a cry of agony in the lobby.

00:58:31.328 --> 00:58:41.605
The fox terrier was evidently satisfied with the result of his experiment, so he decided to continue and create some excitement.

00:58:41.666 --> 00:58:45.888
He jumped over the poodle and attacked the collie.

00:58:45.948 --> 00:58:50.359
Collie woke up and immediately started fighting with the poodle.

00:58:50.419 --> 00:58:53.860
Then the fox terrier returned to his place.

00:58:53.920 --> 00:58:58.721
He bit the ear of the bulldog and tried to throw him across the lobby.

00:58:58.781 --> 00:59:03.912
The bulldog attacked everything he could find, including the porter.

00:59:03.972 --> 00:59:11.898
This gave the dear little fox terrier the opportunity to fight with a brave little Yorkshire terrier.

00:59:11.958 --> 00:59:20.860
Anyone who understands dogs will know that by this time there was a great big fight in the lobby with all the dogs.

00:59:20.920 --> 00:59:27.106
The big dogs fought the big dogs and the little dogs fought the little dogs.

00:59:27.166 --> 00:59:31.820
At times the little dogs bit the legs of the big dogs.

00:59:31.880 --> 00:59:36.626
The lobby was in chaos and the noise was terrible.

00:59:36.686 --> 00:59:40.790
Soon, there was a crowd of people outside the Haymarket stores.

00:59:40.850 --> 00:59:45.196
They asked, is there a religious meeting on site?

00:59:45.256 --> 00:59:48.788
Is someone killing someone else?

00:59:48.848 --> 00:59:50.580
Why?

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:55.696
Some men came with poles and tried to separate the fighting dogs.

00:59:55.756 --> 00:59:58.546
Then, someone called the police.

00:59:58.606 --> 01:00:02.524
During the fighting, the lovely young lady returned.

01:00:02.584 --> 01:00:09.236
She picked up her sweet little dog, who looked at her innocently, and she kissed him.

01:00:09.296 --> 01:00:15.758
My poor little dear, what did those big bad dogs do to you?

01:00:15.818 --> 01:00:25.402
The little fox terrier looked at her and seemed to say, Oh, thank you for saving me from this horrible fight.

01:00:25.462 --> 01:00:35.021
The young lady said, The haymarket stores must not permit big savage dogs to stay with gentle little dogs.

01:00:35.081 --> 01:00:37.042
This is terrible.

01:00:37.102 --> 01:00:40.122
I must talk to someone about this.

01:00:40.183 --> 01:00:45.534
And this is the nature of fox terriers.

01:00:45.594 --> 01:00:49.585
Part 2.

01:00:49.646 --> 01:00:59.788
Montmorency Now, the only thing my fox terrier, Montmorency, and I don't agree on, is cats.

01:00:59.848 --> 01:01:04.840
I like cats, and Montmorency doesn't.

01:01:04.900 --> 01:01:08.764
When I meet a cat, I stop and say hello.

01:01:08.824 --> 01:01:10.935
I touch it gently.

01:01:10.995 --> 01:01:14.488
The cat is happy, and I am too.

01:01:14.548 --> 01:01:19.670
When Montmorency meets a cat, the whole street knows about it.

01:01:19.730 --> 01:01:27.958
A lot of bad words fly through the air, Now, I know that my fox terrier is not really bad.

01:01:28.018 --> 01:01:29.868
It is only his nature.

01:01:29.928 --> 01:01:35.050
But something really unforgettable happened when we were in Marlowe.

01:01:35.110 --> 01:01:41.642
We went swimming before breakfast, and on the way back, Montmorency met a cat.

01:01:41.702 --> 01:01:46.373
As soon as he saw the cat, he barked with happiness.

01:01:46.433 --> 01:01:50.674
The cat walked slowly across the street.

01:01:50.734 --> 01:01:53.684
Montmorency ran after the cat.

01:01:53.744 --> 01:01:56.424
But the cat didn't run.

01:01:56.484 --> 01:02:00.176
He didn't understand that his life was in danger.

01:02:00.236 --> 01:02:04.388
This cat was big and black.

01:02:04.448 --> 01:02:10.381
It had half a tail, half a nose, and only one ear.

01:02:10.441 --> 01:02:13.983
It was a clever street cat.

01:02:14.043 --> 01:02:21.770
Montmorency is a courageous dog, but the cold eyes of that cat terrified him.

01:02:21.830 --> 01:02:28.726
The cat stopped in the middle of the road, and looked at Montmorency with his cold eyes.

01:02:28.786 --> 01:02:35.230
The cat and dog did not speak, but their conversation was probably like this.

01:02:35.290 --> 01:02:36.842
Yes.

01:02:36.902 --> 01:02:38.983
You want me?

01:02:39.043 --> 01:02:42.406
Can I do anything for you?

01:02:42.466 --> 01:02:44.426
No, thanks.

01:02:44.486 --> 01:02:48.727
If you really want something, please tell me.

01:02:48.787 --> 01:02:50.566
No thanks.

01:02:50.626 --> 01:02:52.217
Nothing at all.

01:02:52.278 --> 01:02:54.470
Very kind of you.

01:02:54.530 --> 01:02:57.803
I'm afraid I made a mistake.

01:02:57.862 --> 01:03:00.274
I thought I knew you.

01:03:00.334 --> 01:03:02.476
Sorry I disturbed you.

01:03:02.536 --> 01:03:03.987
Not at all.

01:03:04.048 --> 01:03:05.940
Quite a pleasure.

01:03:06.000 --> 01:03:08.742
Sure you don't want anything now.

01:03:08.802 --> 01:03:09.882
No, no.

01:03:09.942 --> 01:03:11.344
Nothing at all.

01:03:11.404 --> 01:03:13.025
Very kind of you.

01:03:13.085 --> 01:03:14.176
Good morning.

01:03:14.236 --> 01:03:16.064
Good morning.

01:03:16.124 --> 01:03:19.841
The cat got up and walked away.

01:03:19.901 --> 01:03:23.983
Montmorency came back and followed us very quietly.

01:03:24.043 --> 01:03:27.314
He was silent all day long.

01:03:27.374 --> 01:03:35.198
To this day, if you say the word, cats, to Montmorency, he'll stop walking.

01:03:35.258 --> 01:03:43.212
Then he'll look up at you as if to say, oh, please don't.

01:03:43.272 --> 01:03:47.684
Listening activity.

01:03:47.745 --> 01:03:52.788
You will hear only the cat's part of the conversation.

01:03:52.848 --> 01:03:59.143
You must pretend to be Montmorency and act out his part.

01:03:59.203 --> 01:04:01.414
Yes.

01:04:01.474 --> 01:04:03.846
You want me.

01:04:03.906 --> 01:04:15.274
Can I do anything for you?

01:04:15.334 --> 01:04:37.058
If you really want something, please tell me.

01:04:37.118 --> 01:04:47.474
Not at all.

01:04:47.534 --> 01:04:49.946
Quite a pleasure.

01:04:50.006 --> 01:05:01.378
Sure you don't want anything now.

01:05:01.438 --> 01:05:13.222
Good morning.

01:05:13.282 --> 01:05:20.398
You will hear a recording of parts of the original stories in this book.

01:05:20.458 --> 01:05:26.102
Listen and say which extract is from which story?

01:05:26.162 --> 01:05:28.284
1.

01:05:28.344 --> 01:05:31.416
He took a pen and some paper.

01:05:31.476 --> 01:05:47.744
Now, name of the elephant, Hasan ben aliben selim abdala mohamed moist alhamal jam set ji ji boy dulip sultan ebu budpur.

01:05:47.804 --> 01:05:51.779
Very well, given name Jumbo.

01:05:51.839 --> 01:05:53.020
Very well.

01:05:53.080 --> 01:05:54.431
Place of birth.

01:05:54.491 --> 01:05:57.012
The capital city of Siam.

01:05:57.072 --> 01:05:58.443
Parents living?

01:05:58.503 --> 01:05:59.163
No.

01:05:59.223 --> 01:06:00.124
Dead.

01:06:00.184 --> 01:06:03.646
Had they any other issue besides this one?

01:06:03.706 --> 01:06:04.776
None.

01:06:04.836 --> 01:06:08.448
He was an only child.

01:06:08.508 --> 01:06:11.538
Two.

01:06:11.598 --> 01:06:19.671
Do as you like, then, shouted Flannery, pay for him and take him, or don't pay for him and leave him be.

01:06:19.731 --> 01:06:27.724
Rules is rules, Mr. Morehouse, and Mike Flannery's not going to be called down for breaking of them.

01:06:27.784 --> 01:06:38.826
But you everlastingly stupid idiot shouted Mr. Morehouse, madly shaking a flimsy printed book beneath the agent's nose.

01:06:38.886 --> 01:06:44.578
Can't you read it here in your own plain printed rates?

01:06:44.638 --> 01:06:53.381
Pets, domestic, Franklin to West Coast is properly boxed, 25 cents each.

01:06:53.441 --> 01:06:57.772
He threw the book on the counter in disgust.

01:06:57.832 --> 01:07:00.804
What more do you want?

01:07:00.864 --> 01:07:02.456
Aren't they pets?

01:07:02.516 --> 01:07:04.357
Aren't they domestic?

01:07:04.417 --> 01:07:06.678
Aren't they properly boxed?

01:07:06.738 --> 01:07:09.810
What?

01:07:09.870 --> 01:07:19.766
In the high and far off times, the elephant, Obest Beloved, had no trunk.

01:07:19.826 --> 01:07:26.541
He had only a blackish, bulgy nose, as big as a boot.

01:07:26.601 --> 01:07:33.988
that he could wriggle about from side to side, but he couldn't pick up things with it.

01:07:34.048 --> 01:07:53.342
But there was one elephant, a new elephant, an elephant's child, who was full of insatiable curiosity, and that means he asked ever so many questions.

01:07:53.402 --> 01:08:10.409
I remember being in the lobby of the Haymarket Stores one day, and all round about me were dogs, waiting for the return of their owners, who were shopping inside.

01:08:10.469 --> 01:08:31.400
There were a mastiff, and one or two collies, Anderson Bernard, a few retrievers and Newfoundlands, a boarhound, a fringe poodle, with plenty of hair around its head, but mangy about the middle.

01:08:31.460 --> 01:08:42.765
A bulldog, a few lather arcade sort of animals, about the size of rats, and a couple of Yorkshire tikes.

01:08:42.826 --> 01:08:48.989
There they sat, patient, good, and thoughtful.

01:08:49.048 --> 01:08:53.586
A solemn peacefulness.

01:08:53.646 --> 01:09:03.186
It was Mrs. Packletide's pleasure and intention that she should shoot a tiger.

01:09:03.246 --> 01:09:20.197
Not that the lust to kill had suddenly descended on her, or that she felt that she would leave India safer and more wholesome than she had found it, with one fraction less of wild beast per million of inhabitants.